    "q-355831": {
      "dom_id": "step-q355831",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $4$ datapoints with $2$ features in the form of the $4\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-0.27 & 0.39 \\\\ -0.99 & -1.23 \\\\ -0.13 & -0.28 \\\\ 1.39 & 1.12\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}0.035 & 0.86 & 0.132 & 0.491 \\\\ -0.657 & -0.314 & -0.208 & 0.653 \\\\ -0.121 & -0.196 & 0.969 & 0.092 \\\\ 0.743 & -0.351 & -0.032 & 0.569\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.389 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0.542 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.707 & -0.707 \\\\ 0.707 & 0.707\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following matrices corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance?\n\nI. $[\\begin{aligned}0.035 \\\\ -0.657 \\\\ -0.121 \\\\ 0.743\\end{aligned}]\\cdot 2.389$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}0.707 \\\\ 0.707\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $[\\begin{aligned}0.035 \\\\ 0.86 \\\\ 0.132 \\\\ 0.491\\end{aligned}]\\cdot 2.389$",
      "worked_solution": "The one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance is the projection onto the first principal component.\n\nRecall that the principal components are the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix ${X}^{T}X.$\n\nUsing the SVD and the fact that $U$ is orthogonal (i.e., ${U}^{T}U=I$), we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{T}X & =(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}{U}^{T}UΣ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}Σ{V}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $Σ$ is a diagonal matrix, and so is ${Σ}^{T}Σ,$ we know that the eigenvectors of ${X}^{T}X$ are the columns of $V.$\n\nIn particular, the first principal component is the eigenvector of ${X}^{T}X$ corresponding to the largest eigenvalue. \nAnd since $Σ$ contains the corresponding singular values (square roots of the eigenvalues) in decreasing order, the first principal component is ${v}_{1},$ the first column of $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance. Notice that it represents ${u}_{1}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{1},$ where ${u}_{1}$ is the first column of $U$ and ${\\sigma }_{1}$ is the first singular value. But this equals  $X\\cdot {v}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X\\cdot {v}_{1} & =UΣ{V}^{T}\\cdot {v}_{1}=UΣ\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=U\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={u}_{1}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Option II represents ${v}_{1},$ which is the first principal component. It represents a direction in feature space, not a version of the data. Notice that it is also dimensionally incorrect, since it is a $2\\times 1$ matrix instead of $4\\times 1.$\n\n- Option III uses the values of the first row of $U$ multiplied by ${\\sigma }_{1}.$ However, the projection corresponds to the first column of $U.$ Thus, using the row vector is incorrect.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
